WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.600
My name is Tom Birchard, and I am the proud
owner of Veselka restaurant on the corner

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.500
of 2nd Avenue and 9th Street.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:25.360
A pierogi is an Eastern European dumpling
made with a simple dough wrapper and a simple

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.100
filling.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.580
It can be filled with potato, meat, cabbage,
or cheese.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:34.140
It's the Eastern European equivalent of a
ravioli or a Chinese dumpling.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.940
I think every culture has a form of dumpling
that they're known for.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.220
We're known or pierogis.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.620
The pierogi recipe goes back to when I first
came here in the ‘60s.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:52.100
The process starts with making a large vat
of dough, similar to pizza dough.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.960
We run it through a machine called a sheeter
that rolls it out into a fairly thin sheet.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.680
We take that sheet, put it on a table, and
then cut disks out of it.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.640
We're left with a table full of these disks,
and then we put the stuffing in.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.710
Each one is lovingly crimped by hand.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.160
That's where most of the labor comes in.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.580
We make 2,000-3,000 a day, so it's a lot of
hand work.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.090
The pierogis have to be boiled to seal them,
but they're not completely cooked.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:26.020
They’re cooled, packed in containers, and
held until they come upstairs for final preparation,

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.080
which involves another round of boiling or
frying until they're tender.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.580
Then they're served with sour cream and sautéed
onions.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:35.580
Some people like apple sauce.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.580
That’s not traditional.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.280
But sour cream and onions are required.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.960
Pierogis are both Polish and Ukranian.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:51.520
That part of Western Ukraine was governed
by several different political groups.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.542
What we call Ukranian food, other people would
call Polish food, some people would call it

00:01:55.542 --> 00:01:56.709
Jewish food.

00:01:56.709 --> 00:01:59.050
Sometimes I describe it as peasant food.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.050
All those things are true.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.350
This is Eastern European home-cooking.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:08.260
It’s really, really well known and loved
in New York, and by some people around the

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.160
country.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.880
Over the years I've been concerned that this
cuisine would fall out of favor eventually,

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.500
but honestly the opposite has happened.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.970
It seems to be getting more popular.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:27.220
Non-Eastern European or non-Jewish people
really feel an affinity for this food.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:33.390
So that's really gratifying to me—to satisfy
that yearning for grandma's cooking.

